<article class="env-corollary" id="06T9">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06T9">Corollary <span data-tag="06T9">6.2.1.21</span>.</a> Let $G: \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be a functor of $\infty $-categories which admits a left adjoint $F: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$. For every integer $n$, the functor $G$ carries $n$-truncated objects of $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ to $n$-truncated objects of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $Y$ be an $n$-truncated object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$. We wish to show that the object $G(Y) \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is $n$-truncated: that is, that the morphism space $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(X, G(Y) )$ is an $n$-truncated Kan complex for each object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. Using Proposition <a data-tag="02F1" href="/tag/02F1">6.2.1.17</a>, we can identify $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(X, G(Y) )$ with the morphism space $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}}( F(X), Y)$, which is $n$-truncated by virtue of our assumption on $Y$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
